  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">HUMAN-JAGUAR (PANTHERA ONCA) AND HUMAN-OCELOT (LEOPARDUS PARDALIS) CONFLICT IN THE EL CIELO-SIERRA DE TAMALAVE BIOLOGICAL CORRIDOR, TAMAULIPAS, MEXICO</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">THE GOAL OF THIS PROJECT IS THAT BY 2030, HUMAN-JAGUAR AND OCELOT CONFLICT INCIDENTS HAVE DECREASED BY 30% IN THE EL CIELO-SIERRA DE TAMALAVE BIOLOGICAL CORRIDOR OF TAMAULIPAS, MEXICO. THIS PROJECT IS INTENDED TO CONSERVE JAGUARS AND OCELOTS BY ADDRESSING THE IMPACTS OF HABITAT LOSS, POACHING, AND THE LACK OF CAPACITIES OF THE COMMUNITIES TO REPORT PREDATION EVENTS AND APPLY TO THE MEXICAN LIVESTOCK COMPENSATION PROGRAM CURRENTLY AVAILABLE. SPECIFIC PROJECT ACTIVITIES INCLUDE: 1) GENERATE A DIAGNOSIS OF THE HUMAN-CARNIVORE CONFLICT IN THE PROJECT AREA 2) FORMULATE A MODEL OF THE GEOGRAPHICAL DISTRIBUTION OF THE RISK OF THE HUMAN-CARNIVORE CONFLICT 3) DEVELOP A DATABASE WITH RECORDS OF DOMESTIC LIVESTOCK INCIDENTS OF PREDATION BY CARNIVORES ACROSS THE CORRIDOR 4) ESTABLISH A WORKING GROUP COMPOSED OF MAIN ACTORS AND KEY STAKEHOLDERS FOR THE ATTENTION OF CONFLICTS AND PREDATION EVENTS 5) IMPLEMENTATION OF DETERRENT METHODS IN AREAS WITH A HIGH RISK OF PREDATION EVENTS 6) DELIVERY OF THREE AWARENESS AND TRAINING WORKSHOPS FOR LIVESTOCK PRODUCERS TO REDUCE THE HUMAN-CARNIVORE CONFLICT 7) ESTIMATE THE POPULATION SIZE AND ACTIVITY PATTERNS OF THE JAGUAR AND OCELOT, AND 8) DEVELOPMENT OF A CARNIVORE CONSERVATION PLAN.</narrative>
  </description>
